{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1781560851,"nonce":"LDMSSZ+jgLCwAZ4VOd8xuGxc+nk=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABGOfdAA","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/145#issuecomment-4712815872"},"message":"## Field motivation + refinements (2026-06-15 Lake Massabesic wrap-up)\n\nAdding the concrete motivating incident and three design refinements from the operator.\n\n### Motivating field event — under-bridge Starlink loss (2026-06-03)\n\nThe strongest real-world case for this issue isn't captured in the body above. On the 2026-06-03 pier deployment, the boat attempted to transit **under a bridge** (recorded in `docs/logs/2026/2026-06-03_dev_logs.md`):\n\n\u003e **\"Big disappointment: boat could NOT cross under a bridge.\"** … Autonomy not crossing was anticipated (reflex collision_monitor sees the structure). Operator switched to **manual control** to take it under; **under the bridge, lost Starlink, and the boat drifted back** toward where it came from. The limitation is **comms/transit, not planning** — manual helm runs over Starlink, the bridge structure obstructs it, and on link loss there was no propulsion command → drift.\n\nThe 06-03 log flagged \"consider a transit approach that survives loss of Starlink\" as a candidate issue; this is its home. A redundant cell-backed control path is exactly what would have kept helm/heartbeat alive through the obstruction.\n\n**Near-term relevance:** a bridge transit is again under consideration at Lake Massabesic, so this is relevant very soon — not only the August OTH horizon.\n\n### Refinement 1 — replicate the critical set, don't fail over\n\nFor the critical low-rate channel (heartbeat, GPS/position, helm), **send every message over all available paths simultaneously and dedupe at the receiver** (sequence numbers + drop-duplicates), rather than detect-loss-and-switch. The under-bridge failure happened precisely *at* link loss, which is exactly when a failover detection+reconverge gap hurts most. Replication has no switchover hole, and the traffic is a few kbps so triple-sending it costs nothing. Bulk (video, costmap, sonar) stays Starlink-only, **never** cell.\n\n### Refinement 2 — helm over the redundant path is not a new risk\n\nWe already drive **throttle + rudder from the USB joystick over Starlink** and are accustomed to its latency. Replicating helm across Starlink + cell therefore adds redundancy without introducing a new latency regime — it's the same control we already operate, just multi-path.\n\n### Refinement 3 — refined hypothesis on the Verizon WG block\n\nThe body hypothesizes carrier DPI/port-filtering keyed to high-bandwidth UDP. A simpler refinement: the 51820 block may have been triggered by the **high-bandwidth load dumping onto cell at the moment of WAN failover** (an overload/burst event), not the port number per se. This *reinforces* the concurrent-not-failover design — if cell only ever carries the steady low-bitrate critical channel and never absorbs a failover burst, the trigger never occurs.\n\n- **Testable:** sustained low-bitrate UDP on cell should stay up indefinitely; it's the failover-burst that provoked filtering. If a sustained-low test stays up where a burst gets blocked, that confirms overload (not port) as the mechanism.\n\n### Net\n\nDesign in the body stands; this comment adds the bridge incident as the motivating event, narrows the critical path to **replicated** (not failed-over) heartbeat/GPS/helm, and reframes the cell-reliability concern as a failover-burst overload problem that the concurrent design avoids by construction.\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context)`","files":null}]}